 "productionNotes": "No production figure on this page comes from Ford, because every Ford-owned site (media.ford.com, corporate.ford.com, the Heritage Vault) refused automated retrieval in September 2026. What is left is a set of secondary sources that do not agree. The most-repeated total is 4,038 cars over the 2005 and 2006 model years, stated by Sports Car Market (with the breakdown of approximately 550 built in calendar 2004, 1,900 in 2005 and 1,600 in 2006), by Wikipedia, by classic.com and by Supercars.net. RM Sotheby's Miami 2026 catalog states 4,033, and the per-year figures published by Exotic Car Hacks (2,022 for 2005 and 2,011 for 2006) sum to the same 4,033. The 2005 model year is itself contested: Hagerty's buyer's guide and Supercars.net say 2,027, while RM Sotheby's Arizona 2024 catalog and Exotic Car Hacks say 2,022. The 2006 figure of 2,011 is the one number every source that gives it agrees on. The five-car gap between 4,033 and 4,038 is small, but it is real and no source consulted explains it, so productionTotal is left null. The Heritage Edition count is 343 in Hagerty (twice), RM Sotheby's, Sports Car Market, Exotic Car Hacks and the owner-compiled color table Karl Brauer published in November 2006; Wikipedia alone says 346. Two smaller figures are single-sourced and labeled as such: Hagerty says about 200 GTs were sold new in Canada, and RM Sotheby's says 16 of the 2005 cars were stripe-delete Quick Silver. Ford's original plan, per Wikipedia, was 4,500 cars with about 100 for export to Europe; MotorTrend in November 2003 reported Ford expected to sell roughly 1,500 a year. Model-year note for a US reader: the first cars were built in calendar 2004 and titled as 2005 models; NHTSA's recall record gives the GT build window as February 20, 2004 to September 22, 2006, and Wikipedia gives the end of production as September 2006.",
